<MainLayout title="Editorial policy" description="How AI Pulse researches, verifies, and corrects its reporting."><div class="wrap"><header class="page-title"><span class="eyebrow">Our standards</span><h1>Editorial policy</h1><p>Our commitment to accurate, transparent, and useful reporting on artificial intelligence.</p></header><article class="article-body"><h2>Research and sourcing</h2><p>We prioritize primary sources such as research papers, official announcements, filings, and direct statements. Stories distinguish confirmed information from analysis and identify uncertainty when it matters.</p><h2>Use of automation</h2><p>AI tools may assist research and drafting. They do not replace source verification or editorial responsibility. Automated output is checked against its underlying sources before publication.</p><h2>Corrections and updates</h2><p>We update published stories when material facts change or an error is identified. The article’s updated date reflects substantive changes. Readers should use the contact details listed on our contact page to report a concern.</p><h2>Independence and attribution</h2><p>We identify sources, link to original material, and aim to separate reporting from opinion. Any specific funding, sponsorship, or conflict disclosures will be stated on the relevant article.</p></article></div></MainLayout>
